/-!
# Brown representability for Grothendieck abelian categories

Let `A` be a Grothendieck abelian category. A contravariant cohomological functor
`H : D(A)ᵒᵖ ⥤ Ab` which transforms direct sums into products is representable.

The proof is the one of the source. Choose a separator `G` of `A`; the derived Gabriel--Popescu
embedding [Stacks, Tag 0F5V] presents `D(A)` as the image of a fully faithful right adjoint `Rt`
of an exact functor `Fu` out of the derived category of the modules over `End G`, with
`Fu ∘ Rt ≅ id`. That module derived category is generated by the single compact object `R[0]`,
so Brown representability over a compactly generated triangulated category [Stacks, Tag 0A8F]
applies to `H ∘ Fu` and produces an object `L` with `H(Fu -) ≅ Hom(-, L)`. Completing the unit
`η : L ⟶ Rt(Fu L)` to a distinguished triangle `N ⟶ L ⟶ Rt(Fu L) ⟶ N[1]`, the functor `Fu` kills
`N` — `Fu η` is invertible because the counit is — so `Hom(N, L) = H(Fu N) = 0` and the
cohomological long exact sequence of `Hom(-, L)` splits `η`, giving `r` with `η ≫ r = 𝟙`
[Stacks, Tag 05QT]. Then `H` is the retract of `Hom(-, Fu L)` cut out by the idempotent
`r ≫ η`, and `D(A)` is Karoubian [Stacks, Tag 05QW], so that idempotent splits and the summand
represents `H`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Injectives.isRepresentable_of_isHomological_of_isGrothendieckAbelian`: Brown
  representability for the unbounded derived category of a Grothendieck abelian category,
  [Stacks, Tag 0F5X].
* `Stacks.Injectives.isRepresentable_of_isGrothendieckAbelian`: the same under the hypothesis
  `IsGrothendieckAbelian.{max w u v} A`, which only the zero category satisfies.
* `Stacks.Injectives.exists_exact_rightAdjoint`: an exact functor out of that derived category
  which transforms direct sums into direct sums has an exact right adjoint, under
  `IsGrothendieckAbelian.{max w u v} A`, which only the zero category satisfies. The statement of
  [Stacks, Tag 0F5Y] is
  `Stacks.Injectives.exists_exact_rightAdjoint_of_isGrothendieckAbelian`, in
  `StacksFrontier.Homology.Injectives.BrownRepresentabilityRightAdjoint`.

## Implementation notes

*Universes.* The values of `H` lie in `max u v`, the universe of the morphisms of the derived
category constructed by `HasDerivedCategory.standard`, while the chosen model of `D(A)` is
allowed an independent morphism universe `z`; representability is therefore stated for the
underlying type-valued functor, `Functor.RepresentableBy` allowing a bijection between a
morphism type in `Type z` and a value in `Type (max u v)`.

Brown representability is applied over a triangulated category whose morphisms, whose direct-sum
indices and whose `Hom`-values all live in *one* universe, and the proof must put the module
category there. Two reductions do that. First `A` is replaced by `ShrinkHoms A`, whose morphisms
are in `max u v`: the objects are unchanged, so the morphism universe of the module category of
the separator becomes `max u v` as well. Second, the derived category of that module category is
taken in the model with morphisms in `max u v` provided by [Stacks, Tag 079Q] rather than the
standard one, whose morphisms would be one universe too big for the direct sums that exist in a
module category. Both reductions are transported along triangulated equivalences of derived
categories: the first is induced by the exact equivalence `A ≌ ShrinkHoms A`, the second is the
comparison of two localizations of the same homotopy category.

*Compactness.* [Stacks, Tag 0A8F] asks that `Hom(E, -)` preserve direct sums as a functor to
`AddCommGrpCat`, while `CategoryTheory.IsCompactObject` says that the canonical map
`⨁ i, (E ⟶ X i) ⟶ (E ⟶ ∐ X)` is bijective. The two agree, and
`preservesColimitsOfShape_preadditiveCoyoneda_of_isCompactObject` bridges them: bijectivity of
that map is exactly the statement that the image cofan is a colimit cofan, the universal
property being read off from the universal property of a direct sum of abelian groups.

*The right adjoint.* An exact functor `F` out of `D(A)` has a right adjoint as soon as the
contravariant functor `Hom(F -, Y)` is representable for every `Y`, which is Brown
representability applied to it: it is cohomological because `F` is exact, and it turns direct
sums into products because `F` preserves them and `Hom(-, Y)` is a limit-preserving functor on
the opposite category. Exactness of the adjoint is then Mathlib's
`CategoryTheory.Adjunction.isTriangulated_rightAdjoint`, read through the commutation with the
shift that the adjunction induces.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F5X](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F5X)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F5Y](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F5Y)
-/

@[expose] public section

